{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"bdb6a8a9-4c24-4edf-a1bb-774ca039b6e7","agentId":"a58988e040f23c8a4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Repo C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers. Python .venv\\Scripts\\python.exe. No subagents, never open COM3 or call the live adapter; fakes only. Report under 80 words. You may commit and push (remote `github` main only; never `forge`; never koch-mcp/).\n\ncuore/tests/check_coverage.py has 2 failing checks: they assert the legislated OBD reads use the 11-bit STP 33 route, but since commit 1924adf cuore/live/ops._obd_route() prefers the route that answered the active probe (this Stelvio answers legislated requests only on 29-bit STP 34; 11-bit stays the default when no probe has verified). Read ops._obd_route and cuore/live/coverage.py; decide whether the coverage code itself still hard-codes the 11-bit route anywhere (if so, make it call _obd_route()/the same preference, so a coverage pass on this car uses the verified route) and update the two checks to cover both cases: no verification → 11-bit; verified_by active_probe_0100 with probe_stn_protocol \"34\" → 29-bit. Run check_coverage.py (must pass), check_live.py, check_active_probe.py, check_fullscan.py. Commit \"Coverage: legislated reads follow the verified route (29-bit on this Stelvio); tests cover both\" ending with \"Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\", push, report the hash."},"uuid":"7e47bc07-1b5f-4701-96f7-2445e6c37d4d","timestamp":"2026-10-14T05:18:38.566Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers","sessionId":"7cb02eb5-e299-4baf-b34b-9eb82aebbf17","version":"2.1.282","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-splashing-comet"}
